## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to unify log parsing across formats, handle gzip rotation, and implement a time-bounded, deduplicated aggregation with minimal dependencies.

## Key Concepts
- Mixed-format log parsing (text vs JSON)
- Sliding 2-hour window using timestamps
- Deduplication via normalized message fingerprints
- Streaming pipelines with gzip streams
- Per-host aggregation and top-N results

## Code Example
```awk
# skeleton: read lines, manage blocks, normalize, count
BEGIN{IGNORECASE=1}
{ 
  if ($0 ~ /^ERROR:/) {inblock=1; msg=substr($0, index($0, ":")+1); next}
  if (inblock && $0 ~ /^[ 	]+/) {msg = msg" "$0; next}
  if (inblock && $0 !~ /^[ 	]/) {inblock=0; 
    ts=extract_ts(prev); key=tolower(norm(msg)); count[key]++
  }
  if ($0 ~ /^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}/) {prev=$0}
}
END{for (k in count) print k, count[k]}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical UNIX text parsing, time bucketing, and cross-format handling with minimal tooling, plus robustness to unreadable files.

## Key Concepts
- Robust parsing of two log formats
- Time bucketing by hour
- Cross-file aggregation
- Handling unreadable files

## Code Example
```bash
find /var/log -type f -mtime -0.5 -name 'build*.log' -print0 | \
  xargs -0 awk '
    function hour(ts){ return substr(ts,1,13) ":00" }
    {
      if (match($0, /^([0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}) ([0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}) (ERROR|WARN)/, m)) {
        h = hour(m[1]" "m[2]); lvl = toupper(m[3]); c[h":"lvl]++
      } else if (match($0, /"ts":"([^"]+)",.*"lvl":"([^"]+)"/, j)) {
        t = j[1]; gsub(/[-:TZ]/, " ", t); h = substr(t,1,13) ":00"; lvl = toupper(j[2]); c[h":"lvl]++
      }
    }
    END{ for (k in c) print k, c[k] }
  ' \
  | sort
```

